		<div class="post" id="post-1387646">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,1387646)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-1387646">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hornby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1259135); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1259135&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609579071" alt="Hornby" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1259135)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hornby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1259135); return false;" >Hornby</a></span> <span class="odate time_1330907307 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Mar 2012 00:28</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1387646">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I *almost* want to do a TroyL-style blow-by-blow commentary on why this congles mocks, but I couldn't listen to it again.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For the record, I do welcome and appreciate constructive criticism. Accordingly, thank you for your comments.</p>
<p>I recognize that you are senior staff, but I respectfully point out that while constructive criticism is helpful, in my opinion extended and heated rants are not.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The concept of a training exercise that designed to hurt, not kill is outright retarded. They're presumably valuable personnel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Um, what? Yes, personnel would be considered valuable. Just like the military uses MILES preferentially to live-fire training exercises between opposing infantry forces. The theory here is that MTF personnel aren't going up against other infantry, they're going up against dangerous preternatural forces. So, the OPFOR is an anomaly created by the Foundation explicitly for training purposes - the powers that be know how to properly secure and contain it, but since MTF members wouldn't necessarily have all the correct information dealing with a &quot;real&quot; entity that would become an SCP, the officers running the exercise wouldn't necessarily tell the trainees information that is correct or complete. The anomaly is &quot;challenging enough that if [the MTF] screwed up, [they'd] end up in the infirmary – but not the morgue&quot; - precisely what I'd imagine they'd want for training MTF members: unlikely to be lethal, but dangerous enough to convincingly stand in for any given dangerous SCP.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A Colonel supervising a Staff Sergeant, a Corporal and several Specialists?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The Colonel's supervising the exercise. Xi-13 is established as being platoon-strength, so it would be under the overall command of a Lieutenant (who doesn't appear in the audio drama). The Colonel would be supervising because of the inclusion of EVAN in the exercise, as he/it is an experimental technology whose usefulness and effectiveness is being evaluated.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>that's not a squad, by the way</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No, it's not. It's a fireteam - four members, where a squad has eight (two fireteams) plus a squad leader.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No field personnel talk like that, and they don't speak like such - I dunno, &quot;pansies&quot; I guess is the word - to each other.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>MTFs are not US Marines. They might or might not have military backgrounds. This is a headcanon thing, but in my mind I see them as being what TVTropes would refer to as &quot;mildly military.&quot; Just like special forces in real life generally are permitted slightly more relaxed discipline in circumstances where discipline is less strictly necessary (i.e., out of combat operations). The conversation at the beginning thus falls into what I would consider an &quot;acceptable break from reality.&quot; Also, I was deliberately trying to establish that the structure does weird things, messing with the heads of those inside. Why? Well, that's open to interpretation. I have an answer, but it's more fun to leave it up to people.</p>

							</div>

								
					
